WEBVTT

1
00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.880
Picture Earth about three billion years ago.

2
00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:11.920
There are oceans, volcanoes and sunlight.

3
00:00:12.700 --> 00:00:14.700
But there is almost no oxygen in the air.

4
00:00:17.183 --> 00:00:21.183
The sky may have been hazy, perhaps tinted orange by a methane haze.

5
00:00:22.103 --> 00:00:24.683
Nobody saw it, so we rebuild it from ancient rocks.

6
00:00:27.217 --> 00:00:31.077
The oceans held dissolved iron, in its reduced, ferrous form.

7
00:00:31.877 --> 00:00:36.177
With little oxygen to take its electrons, much of that iron stayed dissolved.

8
00:00:38.800 --> 00:00:41.680
Life was already here, and all of it was microbial.

9
00:00:42.940 --> 00:00:46.120
Cells likely made energy by fermenting or by making methane.

10
00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.400
Some may have reduced sulfate.

11
00:00:50.783 --> 00:00:55.503
Every one of those lifestyles moves electrons from a donor to an acceptor.

12
00:00:56.343 --> 00:00:58.863
Oxygen, a far more powerful acceptor, was scarce.

13
00:01:01.433 --> 00:01:05.853
The land had no plants and no animals, though microbes may have crusted damp

14
00:01:05.853 --> 00:01:09.473
patches. Plants would not arrive for over two billion years.

15
00:01:15.967 --> 00:01:17.307
Then came a new trick.

16
00:01:18.347 --> 00:01:22.267
Cyanobacteria learned to pull electrons out of water, powered by sunlight.

17
00:01:23.147 --> 00:01:24.907
The waste product was oxygen gas.

18
00:01:27.450 --> 00:01:29.450
Water holds its electrons tightly.

19
00:01:30.610 --> 00:01:35.030
Photosystem two splits it, and photosystem one then boosts the electrons higher.

20
00:01:35.990 --> 00:01:40.010
This machinery evolved once, in the ancestors of cyanobacteria.

21
00:01:41.270 --> 00:01:44.030
Plants later gained it by taking in a cyanobacterium.

22
00:01:46.483 --> 00:01:49.963
In shallow seas, these cells spread as glowing, sticky mats.

23
00:01:51.243 --> 00:01:55.463
Similar mats still build layered mounds, called stromatolites, in some bays and

24
00:01:55.463 --> 00:01:56.463
lakes today.

25
00:01:58.467 --> 00:02:04.447
Predict. If cyanobacteria were releasing oxygen, why did the air not fill with it

26
00:02:04.447 --> 00:02:05.447
right away?

27
00:02:12.417 --> 00:02:15.097
Because oxygen had many places to go.

28
00:02:15.637 --> 00:02:20.777
Dissolved iron handed its electrons to oxygen, formed rust-like minerals, and sank.

29
00:02:26.317 --> 00:02:30.357
Layer by layer, iron and silica built banded iron formations.

30
00:02:31.337 --> 00:02:34.917
Some layers may record microbes rusting iron without oxygen.

31
00:02:35.917 --> 00:02:38.117
Much of the iron we mine today comes from them.

32
00:02:40.550 --> 00:02:44.370
Their biggest pulse came just before and as oxygen began to rise.

33
00:02:46.883 --> 00:02:48.563
Iron was not the only sink.

34
00:02:49.503 --> 00:02:53.823
Volcanic gases, methane and minerals on land all consumed oxygen too.

35
00:02:56.317 --> 00:03:01.877
Cyanobacteria most likely arose well before oxygen built up, though by how long is

36
00:03:01.877 --> 00:03:06.817
debated. Some rocks hint at brief whiffs of oxygen before that lasting rise.

37
00:03:09.350 --> 00:03:14.310
Then, between about two point four and two point one billion years ago, oxygen rose,

38
00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.250
wavered, and finally stayed.

39
00:03:17.330 --> 00:03:19.690
Geologists call this the Great Oxygenation Event.

40
00:03:22.183 --> 00:03:25.183
The methane haze may have thinned, leaving a clearer sky.

41
00:03:26.183 --> 00:03:30.223
Even so, oxygen stayed far below today's level for a very long time.

42
00:03:35.717 --> 00:03:37.877
Was there a mass die-off of anaerobes?

43
00:03:38.497 --> 00:03:40.957
It is often said, but the evidence is thin.

44
00:03:41.977 --> 00:03:45.937
Anaerobes clearly survived, in mud, deep water and other airless places.

45
00:03:48.550 --> 00:03:51.230
They thrive there still, including in your own gut.

46
00:03:54.233 --> 00:03:56.333
Oxygen did not end the old world of life.

47
00:03:57.213 --> 00:03:58.333
It pushed it into the shadows.

48
00:04:02.917 --> 00:04:05.077
Why should oxygen be dangerous at all?

49
00:04:06.157 --> 00:04:09.217
Oxygen strongly pulls in four electrons, to become water.

50
00:04:11.750 --> 00:04:15.910
But its two unpaired electrons make it slow to react with most molecules.

51
00:04:16.890 --> 00:04:18.970
It tends to take electrons one at a time.

52
00:04:21.433 --> 00:04:27.113
Predict. If oxygen picks up electrons one at a time, what forms along the way?

53
00:04:34.617 --> 00:04:36.197
Partly reduced oxygen.

54
00:04:37.057 --> 00:04:38.757
One electron makes superoxide.

55
00:04:39.557 --> 00:04:41.157
Two make hydrogen peroxide.

56
00:04:41.977 --> 00:04:44.477
Three make a hydroxyl radical, plus water.

57
00:04:45.397 --> 00:04:47.637
That radical attacks almost anything it touches.

58
00:04:52.750 --> 00:04:55.510
Together these are called reactive oxygen species.

59
00:04:56.490 --> 00:05:01.490
They break DNA, damage proteins, and set off chain reactions in membrane lipids.

60
00:05:03.933 --> 00:05:05.153
Iron makes it worse.

61
00:05:06.253 --> 00:05:11.133
Ferrous iron hands an electron to hydrogen peroxide, releasing the hydroxyl radical.

62
00:05:13.717 --> 00:05:18.637
This is one reason cells keep most of their iron tightly bound, much of it stored

63
00:05:18.637 --> 00:05:19.637
inside proteins.

64
00:05:22.267 --> 00:05:24.767
Here is the idea that ties this film together.

65
00:05:25.687 --> 00:05:29.187
Every way of living with oxygen is a way of handling its electrons.

66
00:05:35.450 --> 00:05:37.450
Cells evolved and spread defenses.

67
00:05:38.670 --> 00:05:42.510
Superoxide dismutase turns superoxide into hydrogen peroxide.

68
00:05:43.530 --> 00:05:46.590
Catalase turns hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen.

69
00:05:49.183 --> 00:05:52.223
Some bacteria went further and put oxygen to work.

70
00:05:53.083 --> 00:05:55.943
They placed it at the end of an electron-transport chain.

71
00:05:58.417 --> 00:06:01.137
Oxygen is the strongest common acceptor.

72
00:06:01.577 --> 00:06:06.117
So an electron that falls all the way to oxygen releases much more energy.

73
00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:14.540
Fermenting glucose to lactate or ethanol yields two A T P. Respiring it fully with

74
00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:17.520
oxygen, as our cells do, yields around thirty.

75
00:06:20.083 --> 00:06:21.763
But the chain is not perfect.

76
00:06:22.623 --> 00:06:27.143
A small fraction of electrons slip off early, often at complexes one and three,

77
00:06:27.503 --> 00:06:28.503
making superoxide.

78
00:06:35.067 --> 00:06:38.227
Now move forward, to some time after oxygen rose.

79
00:06:39.167 --> 00:06:43.547
The oldest widely accepted eukaryote fossils are more than one and a half billion

80
00:06:43.547 --> 00:06:44.547
years old.

81
00:06:46.767 --> 00:06:51.607
The host was most likely an archaeon, related to today's Asgard archaea.

82
00:06:52.207 --> 00:06:54.527
Whether it already had a nucleus is still debated.

83
00:06:57.150 --> 00:07:01.310
Asgard archaea were first found as DNA in deep sea sediments.

84
00:07:02.170 --> 00:07:05.430
Their genomes carry many genes once thought unique to eukaryotes.

85
00:07:07.900 --> 00:07:11.840
The guest was a bacterium related to today's alphaproteobacteria.

86
00:07:12.700 --> 00:07:15.500
It could probably respire, and may have used oxygen.

87
00:07:18.100 --> 00:07:21.300
Somehow, the archaeon came to enclose the bacterium.

88
00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:24.240
And instead of being digested, the guest stayed.

89
00:07:29.750 --> 00:07:32.770
Gene trees suggest that this happened only once.

90
00:07:33.410 --> 00:07:36.650
Every mitochondrion today descends from that single guest.

91
00:07:39.150 --> 00:07:42.270
Most mitochondria still show their bacterial past.

92
00:07:43.130 --> 00:07:48.530
They have two membranes, their own DNA, bacteria-like ribosomes, and they divide by

93
00:07:48.530 --> 00:07:49.530
pinching in two.

94
00:07:51.833 --> 00:07:53.433
Why did the partnership begin?

95
00:07:54.313 --> 00:07:56.553
The honest answer is that it is still debated.

96
00:07:59.133 --> 00:08:02.713
The hydrogen hypothesis says the host lived on hydrogen.

97
00:08:03.613 --> 00:08:07.253
The bacterium gave off hydrogen as waste, so the two fed each other.

98
00:08:09.733 --> 00:08:14.193
The oxygen detoxification hypothesis says oxygen harmed the host.

99
00:08:15.033 --> 00:08:18.073
The bacterium consumed oxygen and shielded its partner.

100
00:08:20.633 --> 00:08:23.573
Both ideas fit some evidence, and neither is proven.

101
00:08:24.513 --> 00:08:28.933
The first Asgard archaeon grown in a lab gives hydrogen to partner microbes.

102
00:08:29.873 --> 00:08:34.113
That is the reverse of the proposed host, but it shows such partnerships are real.

103
00:08:36.567 --> 00:08:39.727
Oxygen changed the world the partners lived in.

104
00:08:40.067 --> 00:08:43.547
But eukaryote fossils appear long after the oxygen rise.

105
00:08:44.447 --> 00:08:46.987
How much oxygen drove their merger is still debated.

106
00:08:49.550 --> 00:08:51.870
Over time, the guest became an organelle.

107
00:08:52.710 --> 00:08:55.830
Many of its genes were lost, and others moved to the nucleus.

108
00:08:58.383 --> 00:09:00.503
But it kept a small genome of its own.

109
00:09:01.403 --> 00:09:07.073
Human mitochondrial DNA encodes just thirteen proteins, all for respiration and A T

110
00:09:07.073 --> 00:09:08.073
P synthesis.

111
00:09:10.317 --> 00:09:16.317
One influential idea is that many mitochondria let eukaryotic cells afford larger

112
00:09:16.317 --> 00:09:18.557
genomes and more complex lives.

113
00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:24.640
So think back to the idea.

114
00:09:25.860 --> 00:09:29.460
Rust, poison and power are all ways of handling oxygen's electrons.

115
00:09:32.033 --> 00:09:36.793
In this lesson, follow electrons from N A D H down the chain to oxygen.

116
00:09:37.773 --> 00:09:41.553
Watch complex four hold oxygen until it is fully reduced to water.

117
00:09:44.117 --> 00:09:45.877
That careful grip is the bargain.

118
00:09:46.797 --> 00:09:51.497
Every breath you take delivers oxygen to a chain inherited from that ancient guest.
